  "articleBody": "Getting your business seen by AI answer engines can feel like navigating a maze, and it's easy to stumble into common traps. Many businesses mistakenly think they just need a quick fix or a 'special AI plugin,' but that's rarely the case. The truth is, some of the biggest roadblocks to AI visibility are things you might not even realize you're doing, like blocking important content or using structured data incorrectly. It's not about tricking AI; it's about making your content genuinely accessible and understandable to it.\n\n### Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n- **Blocking useful pages with `robots.txt` or `noindex`** — This is a classic. You might think you're tidying up, but you could be inadvertently telling AI crawlers to ignore the very pages you want them to cite! Ensure essential content is always crawlable. \n\n- **Assuming a special 'AI file' or proprietary schema is needed** — There’s no magic AI-only markup. Google explicitly states that new machine-readable AI files aren't required. Stick to standard, well-implemented schema.\n\n- **Adding structured data that doesn't match visible content** — This is a big no-no. If your schema says one thing but your page says another, AI systems get confused. Structured data must accurately reflect what users see on the page. \n\n- **Treating crawler access as a guarantee of citation** — Just because AI can crawl your site doesn't mean it will cite you. Crawlability is step one; content quality and relevance for direct answers are key to being chosen. \n\n- **Publishing large volumes of lightly edited AI-generated content** — Google's guidelines emphasize helpful, reliable, people-first content. Quantity over quality, especially with AI-generated text, can hurt your standing.\n\n- **Hiding key facts in images or interactive elements** — AI systems need crawlable text. If your crucial business details are embedded in images or require complex JavaScript to load, AI might miss them entirely.\n\n- **Creating multiple overlapping pages with similar content** — This can dilute the authority of your answers. Aim for one clear, authoritative page per question to avoid confusing AI systems about which information is primary.\n\n### When Professional Guidance Helps\n\nIf you find your site is struggling with widespread indexation issues, complex CMS setups, or if you're dealing with technically intricate content, that's a clear sign it's time to bring in a technical SEO specialist or content strategist. They can help untangle those complexities and make sure your valuable content isn't lost in the digital shuffle, ensuring your business is structured correctly for AI consumption.",
